WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:06.604
Alright, well, let's start
with an easy question.

00:00:06.604 --> 00:00:09.140
How many of you are wearing
a Fitbit or an Apple Watch

00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:11.675
or some other kind
of health tracking device?

00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:14.745
And how many of you have got
a smartphone with you here today?

00:00:14.745 --> 00:00:16.814
Maybe I should say
how many of you have not?

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:21.185
The fact that so many of us have
these technological marvels in our pockets

00:00:21.218 --> 00:00:22.953
or on our body

00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:27.158
is a sure sign of the revolution
that's taking place in computing

00:00:27.158 --> 00:00:28.692
over the last decade.

00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:30.761
And I want you to think
with me for a second

00:00:30.795 --> 00:00:33.164
about the elements of that revolution.

00:00:33.164 --> 00:00:35.132
So first off, are the data.

00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:39.136
These devices are collecting data
about our health, our movements,

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.038
our habits and more.

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:42.373
And what's really important

00:00:42.406 --> 00:00:45.743
is that those data
are not generic population data,

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:48.245
but they're data
that are personalized to us,

00:00:48.279 --> 00:00:50.047
each as an individual.

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:53.284
Second, and just as important,
are the models.

00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:56.020
Inside these devices are very powerful

00:00:56.053 --> 00:00:58.689
mathematical and statistical models.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:02.059
Some of these models
are learned entirely from data,

00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:05.296
perhaps a machine-learning model
that has learned to classify

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:08.933
whether I'm running
or walking or biking or sleeping.

00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:11.602
Some of these models are based in physics,

00:01:11.635 --> 00:01:15.906
such as a physiological model
that describes the equations

00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:19.977
that represent cardiac function
or circadian rhythm.

00:01:20.444 --> 00:01:23.147
And now where things
get really interesting

00:01:23.147 --> 00:01:26.750
is when we start to put
the data and the models together.

00:01:26.784 --> 00:01:30.221
Mathematically, this is known
as data assimilation.

00:01:30.221 --> 00:01:33.057
So we have data and we have models.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.694
With data assimilation,
we start updating the models

00:01:36.694 --> 00:01:39.396
as new data are collected from the system.

00:01:39.396 --> 00:01:43.834
And we don't do this update just once,
but we do it continually.

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:45.870
So as the system changes,

00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:48.572
as I get older and my circadian rhythm

00:01:48.606 --> 00:01:51.742
or as my cardiac function
is not what it once was,

00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:54.578
the new data is collected
and the models are evolving

00:01:54.578 --> 00:01:56.547
and following along with me.

00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:59.350
Now that data assimilation
is really important

00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:03.420
because it's what personalizes
the models to me

00:02:03.454 --> 00:02:06.157
and that then gets us
to the fourth element,

00:02:06.157 --> 00:02:08.292
which is the element of prediction.

00:02:08.325 --> 00:02:10.895
Now that I have these personalized models,

00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:12.596
it's so powerful

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.634
because I can now get predictions
or recommendations

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:19.270
that are tailored to me as an individual

00:02:19.303 --> 00:02:23.607
and that are tailored to my dynamically
evolving state over my life.

00:02:24.008 --> 00:02:26.143
So ... what I’m describing,

00:02:26.143 --> 00:02:29.146
this working together of data and models,

00:02:29.146 --> 00:02:31.815
is likely very familiar to all of you

00:02:31.815 --> 00:02:35.986
because it's been driving your personal
choices in retail and entertainment

00:02:35.986 --> 00:02:38.122
and wellness for many years.

00:02:38.122 --> 00:02:39.456
But what you might not know

00:02:39.456 --> 00:02:42.359
is that a similar revolution
has been taking place

00:02:42.393 --> 00:02:44.528
in engineering systems.

00:02:44.562 --> 00:02:47.898
And in engineering systems,
the story is much the same.

00:02:47.898 --> 00:02:51.802
We have data and we have
increasing amounts of data

00:02:51.802 --> 00:02:57.141
as sensors have become smaller, lighter,
cheaper and more powerful.

00:02:57.741 --> 00:03:00.044
In engineering, we also have models.

00:03:00.077 --> 00:03:02.746
Our models are usually
grounded in physics.

00:03:02.746 --> 00:03:06.650
These models represent
the governing laws of nature.

00:03:06.684 --> 00:03:08.953
They're powerful models
that let us predict

00:03:08.986 --> 00:03:11.956
how an engineering system will respond.

00:03:12.423 --> 00:03:16.660
What you see up here on the slide
is a picture of the unmanned aircraft

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.229
that I have in my research group

00:03:18.229 --> 00:03:21.398
that we use for a great deal
of our research.

00:03:21.398 --> 00:03:22.967
And for this aircraft,

00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:26.170
we have powerful finite element models

00:03:26.203 --> 00:03:29.974
that let us predict
how the aircraft structure will respond

00:03:30.007 --> 00:03:31.508
under different conditions.

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:34.278
So these models let us answer questions

00:03:34.278 --> 00:03:39.316
like, will the structure of the aircraft
hold together on takeoff

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.285
if I design it in this way?

00:03:41.318 --> 00:03:44.088
Or, what happens if the aircraft
wing gets damaged

00:03:44.121 --> 00:03:46.056
and I continue to fly it aggressively?

00:03:46.056 --> 00:03:48.559
Will the aircraft hold together?

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:52.963
And again, just like the Fitbit
and the smartphone example,

00:03:52.963 --> 00:03:56.200
we can put the data
and the models together

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.436
to build a personalized model
of the engineering system,

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.972
a personalized model of the aircraft.

00:04:02.006 --> 00:04:05.342
And we call this personalized
model a digital twin.

00:04:05.776 --> 00:04:07.578
So what is a digital twin?

00:04:07.578 --> 00:04:09.513
It is a personalized,

00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:14.285
dynamically evolving model
of a physical system.

00:04:14.318 --> 00:04:19.423
And I want you to think
about the digital twin of my aircraft.

00:04:19.423 --> 00:04:21.558
So as I create that digital twin,

00:04:21.592 --> 00:04:26.597
I'm going to be collecting data
from the sensors on board the aircraft.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.199
I'm going to be collecting
data from inspections

00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:31.268
I might make of the aircraft,

00:04:31.302 --> 00:04:34.705
and I'm going to be assimilating
that data into the models.

00:04:34.738 --> 00:04:36.040
And what's really important

00:04:36.073 --> 00:04:41.745
is that I’m not building a generic model
of just any old Telemaster aircraft.

00:04:41.779 --> 00:04:43.747
I am building a personalized model

00:04:43.781 --> 00:04:47.184
of the very aircraft that is right now
sitting in my garage

00:04:47.217 --> 00:04:49.253
down the road in South Austin.

00:04:49.253 --> 00:04:52.323
And so that digital twin
will capture the differences,

00:04:52.356 --> 00:04:57.261
the variability from my aircraft
to say, my neighbor's aircraft.

00:04:57.294 --> 00:05:00.264
And what's more, that digital
twin will not be static.

00:05:00.297 --> 00:05:05.502
It's going to change
as my aircraft ages and degrades

00:05:05.502 --> 00:05:07.805
and gets damaged and gets repaired.

00:05:07.838 --> 00:05:10.407
We will be assimilating data all the time

00:05:10.441 --> 00:05:13.977
and the digital twin will follow
the aircraft through its life.

00:05:13.977 --> 00:05:15.479
So this is incredibly powerful.

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:17.748
I want you to imagine now
that you're an airline

00:05:17.781 --> 00:05:20.718
or maybe in a few years’ time,
you’re an operator

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:24.321
of a fleet of unmanned
cargo delivery drones,

00:05:24.321 --> 00:05:27.157
and imagine that you would have
a digital twin like this

00:05:27.191 --> 00:05:29.660
for every vehicle in your fleet.

00:05:29.693 --> 00:05:32.696
And think about what that would mean
for your decision making.

00:05:32.696 --> 00:05:37.768
You could make decisions
about when to maintain any one aircraft,

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:41.605
depending on the particular
evolving state of that aircraft.

00:05:41.605 --> 00:05:45.776
You could make decisions
about how to optimally fly an aircraft

00:05:45.809 --> 00:05:47.444
on any given day,

00:05:47.478 --> 00:05:50.647
given the health of the aircraft,
given the mission needs,

00:05:50.647 --> 00:05:52.349
given the environmental conditions.

00:05:52.349 --> 00:05:55.719
It would really let you optimally
manage that fleet of aircraft.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:00.591
So this idea of a digital twin
is pretty neat.

00:06:00.624 --> 00:06:06.130
The term “digital twin” was coined
in 2010 in a NASA report.

00:06:06.163 --> 00:06:08.832
But the idea, this idea
of a personalized model

00:06:08.832 --> 00:06:12.302
combining models and data, is much older.

00:06:12.636 --> 00:06:15.773
And many people
point to the Apollo program

00:06:15.773 --> 00:06:17.975
as being one of the places

00:06:18.008 --> 00:06:21.779
where digital twins
were first put into practice.

00:06:21.779 --> 00:06:25.282
So in the Apollo program,
back in the '60s and the '70s,

00:06:25.282 --> 00:06:28.952
NASA would launch Apollo
spacecraft up into space,

00:06:28.986 --> 00:06:31.088
and they would also deploy a simulator,

00:06:31.121 --> 00:06:34.224
a virtual model on the ground in Houston,

00:06:34.258 --> 00:06:36.193
to follow along on the mission.

00:06:36.226 --> 00:06:38.328
And now this became very important

00:06:38.328 --> 00:06:41.398
and it became very useful
in the Apollo 13 mission.

00:06:41.398 --> 00:06:45.002
And again, perhaps you all know the story
because we've seen the movie.

00:06:45.002 --> 00:06:49.540
In the Apollo 13 mission,
the spacecraft suffered a malfunction.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.475
It was very badly damaged.

00:06:51.508 --> 00:06:53.710
It became stranded up in space.

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:58.782
And so the story goes that NASA were able
to take the data from the real aircraft,

00:06:58.816 --> 00:07:01.318
the physical twin stuck up in space,

00:07:01.351 --> 00:07:05.055
feed it into the simulator
and to the virtual models

00:07:05.055 --> 00:07:06.490
on the ground in Houston,

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:08.125
do the data assimilation,

00:07:08.158 --> 00:07:10.160
dynamically evolve the simulator

00:07:10.194 --> 00:07:14.231
so now that it represented
the conditions of the damaged spacecraft

00:07:14.231 --> 00:07:18.101
and then use that simulator
to run predictions

00:07:18.135 --> 00:07:19.937
and ultimately guide the decisions

00:07:19.937 --> 00:07:22.639
that brought the astronauts
back home safely.

00:07:23.273 --> 00:07:25.576
So more than 50 years later,

00:07:25.609 --> 00:07:27.978
this idea now has a really great name,

00:07:27.978 --> 00:07:29.847
the name of digital twins.

00:07:29.847 --> 00:07:31.815
And what's really exciting

00:07:31.815 --> 00:07:36.487
is that it's moving well beyond
just aerospace engineering.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.222
So in our engineered world,

00:07:38.255 --> 00:07:42.659
we're starting to see digital twins
of bridges and other civil infrastructure

00:07:42.693 --> 00:07:45.562
for structural health monitoring
and predictive maintenance.

00:07:45.963 --> 00:07:49.766
We're starting to see digital twins
of buildings for energy efficiency,

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.802
digital twins of wind farms

00:07:51.802 --> 00:07:54.538
to increase efficiency
and to reduce downtime.

00:07:55.038 --> 00:07:56.373
In the natural world,

00:07:56.373 --> 00:08:00.811
there's a lot of interest
in creating digital twins of forests,

00:08:00.844 --> 00:08:05.415
farms, ice sheets,
coastal regions, oil reservoirs

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:09.086
and even talk of trying to create
a digital twin of planet Earth.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.021
And in the medical world,

00:08:11.054 --> 00:08:14.992
there's a great deal of interest
in creating digital twins

00:08:15.025 --> 00:08:17.060
to help guide medical assessment,

00:08:17.094 --> 00:08:21.765
diagnosis, personalized treatment
and in silico drug testing.

00:08:22.399 --> 00:08:28.305
So, many, many exciting
potential applications of digital twins.

00:08:28.338 --> 00:08:30.841
But now, I would not like you
to leave my talk today

00:08:30.841 --> 00:08:32.809
thinking that all of this is a reality,

00:08:32.843 --> 00:08:36.680
that we can create digital twins today
of all those complex systems.

00:08:37.014 --> 00:08:42.653
It's still beyond reach to create
a digital twin of an entire aircraft.

00:08:42.686 --> 00:08:46.657
It's still beyond reach to create
a digital twin of a cancer patient

00:08:46.657 --> 00:08:48.625
or of planet Earth.

00:08:49.226 --> 00:08:52.896
Creating digital twins of these very,
very complex systems

00:08:52.896 --> 00:08:55.198
is very, very challenging.

00:08:55.232 --> 00:08:58.435
And let's think for a minute
why it's so challenging.

00:08:58.468 --> 00:09:01.772
So one reason it's very difficult
is because of the scales

00:09:01.805 --> 00:09:03.674
that these systems cross.

00:09:03.707 --> 00:09:06.109
If you think about my aircraft,

00:09:06.109 --> 00:09:08.545
damage at the microscopic level

00:09:08.579 --> 00:09:11.214
on the material on the wing
of the aircraft

00:09:11.214 --> 00:09:13.116
translates across scale

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:16.587
to impact the way the vehicle flies
at the vehicle level.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.457
In medicine, we all know that, again,

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.426
changes at the very fine level,

00:09:22.459 --> 00:09:25.796
at the molecular or the cellular
level in our bodies

00:09:25.796 --> 00:09:29.566
translate across scales to have impacts
on us at the system level,

00:09:29.566 --> 00:09:31.301
at the human level.

00:09:31.335 --> 00:09:34.938
And computational models
that resolve all of these scales,

00:09:34.972 --> 00:09:38.408
from the microscale all the way up
to the system level,

00:09:38.442 --> 00:09:39.977
are computationally intractable.

00:09:39.977 --> 00:09:43.146
We can't solve them even with today's
supercomputing power.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.450
But then you might say,
"OK, well what about the data?

00:09:46.483 --> 00:09:47.918
You said we had a lot of data.

00:09:47.951 --> 00:09:50.887
Can we not just learn
digital twins from data?"

00:09:51.321 --> 00:09:54.091
So yes, we live in an era of big data

00:09:54.091 --> 00:09:56.827
and we have a lot of data
often for our systems.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.663
But when it comes
to these very challenging,

00:09:59.663 --> 00:10:03.867
complex systems in engineering,
in science and in medicine,

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.738
the data by themselves
are almost never enough.

00:10:07.738 --> 00:10:13.176
The data are almost always very sparse
in both space and in time.

00:10:13.176 --> 00:10:17.114
The data are almost always noisy
and they're indirect.

00:10:17.147 --> 00:10:18.281
As an engineer,

00:10:18.315 --> 00:10:21.118
I can almost never measure
what it is I want to know.

00:10:21.151 --> 00:10:24.855
If I want to know about the health
on the structure inside my aircraft wing,

00:10:24.888 --> 00:10:27.324
I can't just break it open
and take a look.

00:10:27.357 --> 00:10:31.595
I am limited to those few sensors
that are on the surface of the wing,

00:10:31.628 --> 00:10:34.531
taking those measurements
and then trying to guess.

00:10:34.564 --> 00:10:38.669
More than guess, trying to infer
what's happening inside the wing.

00:10:38.702 --> 00:10:40.570
The same is true in medicine.

00:10:40.604 --> 00:10:44.808
A medical practitioner can't open
somebody up to take a look at an organ.

00:10:44.841 --> 00:10:47.077
Again, we are limited to sparse,

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:51.248
noisy and indirect observations
taken from the outside

00:10:51.248 --> 00:10:53.550
to try to infer what's going on.

00:10:54.151 --> 00:10:55.318
So then you might say,

00:10:55.352 --> 00:10:57.220
"Well, we just have to wait a few years

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.357
because sensing technology will get
better and better and better."

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:01.758
And that's true.

00:11:01.792 --> 00:11:04.695
Maybe, maybe then we'll have enough data

00:11:04.728 --> 00:11:06.563
to really be able to characterize

00:11:06.563 --> 00:11:09.666
what is going on inside
these very complex systems.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:11.268
But even that's not enough,

00:11:11.268 --> 00:11:15.072
because all that would tell us
is what's happening now.

00:11:15.105 --> 00:11:17.107
And remember, we have
to do more than that.

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:20.510
We have to be able to predict
what might happen in the future

00:11:20.544 --> 00:11:22.779
if we take different actions.

00:11:22.779 --> 00:11:25.248
So we're always going to need the models.

00:11:25.615 --> 00:11:29.419
So this sounds like a huge
challenge, and indeed it is.

00:11:29.419 --> 00:11:34.257
But the good news is that we have a lot
of hope for addressing this challenge.

00:11:34.291 --> 00:11:35.859
And a big part of this hope

00:11:35.859 --> 00:11:39.763
rests on this notion of predictive
physics-based models.

00:11:39.796 --> 00:11:43.066
These are the models that encode
the governing laws of nature

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.302
that let us make predictions --

00:11:45.335 --> 00:11:48.338
predict how a cancer tumor might grow

00:11:48.371 --> 00:11:51.975
or how a cancer tumor might respond
to radiotherapy treatment,

00:11:52.008 --> 00:11:55.545
or predict how an Antarctic
ice sheet might flow

00:11:55.545 --> 00:11:58.882
under different future
temperature scenarios.

00:11:58.915 --> 00:12:02.252
And bringing these predictive
physics-based models together

00:12:02.252 --> 00:12:04.488
with powerful machine learning,

00:12:04.488 --> 00:12:07.090
with scalable methods and data simulation

00:12:07.124 --> 00:12:09.392
and optimization and decision making,

00:12:09.426 --> 00:12:11.528
and with high performance computing,

00:12:11.561 --> 00:12:15.165
that's the realm of the interdisciplinary
field of computational science,

00:12:15.198 --> 00:12:17.467
and that's the focus of the Oden Institute

00:12:17.501 --> 00:12:19.069
here at UT Austin,

00:12:19.102 --> 00:12:20.604
where we bring together faculty

00:12:20.604 --> 00:12:23.173
from 24 different
departments across campus

00:12:23.206 --> 00:12:26.143
to tackle these kinds
of challenging problems.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.280
So I’m going to close
by provoking your imagination

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:33.950
and I hope you’re excited, like I am,
about the idea of a digital twin.

00:12:33.984 --> 00:12:36.620
And maybe as you go home,
you can look around and think,

00:12:36.653 --> 00:12:38.722
"Oh, what if we had
a digital twin of that?"

00:12:38.722 --> 00:12:42.993
But let's look at some examples
of some of the really exciting areas

00:12:43.026 --> 00:12:45.595
where digital twins
could make a difference

00:12:45.595 --> 00:12:48.231
in tackling some of the biggest
problems facing society.

00:12:48.265 --> 00:12:49.466
And as I go through this,

00:12:49.499 --> 00:12:51.935
you'll also see some
of the really exciting research

00:12:51.968 --> 00:12:54.237
that we have going on here at UT Austin.

00:12:54.271 --> 00:12:56.973
So the first area is space systems.

00:12:57.007 --> 00:13:00.443
You probably all know,
we are at the dawn of a new space era.

00:13:00.477 --> 00:13:03.346
It is so exciting and it's
so exciting for our students.

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:06.116
And what's even more exciting
is that central Texas

00:13:06.116 --> 00:13:09.119
is right in the midst of that new era.

00:13:09.152 --> 00:13:14.491
So digital twins clearly have a role
to play in managing the health

00:13:14.524 --> 00:13:19.396
and the operations of space systems,
of launch vehicles, of satellites.

00:13:19.429 --> 00:13:21.398
You can see here,
this is some of the work

00:13:21.398 --> 00:13:24.601
that I'm doing together with my
colleagues from the Cockrell School,

00:13:24.601 --> 00:13:27.003
Renato Zanetti and Srinivas Bettadpur.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:30.974
Digital twins also have
a big role to play in tracking

00:13:30.974 --> 00:13:34.010
and managing space objects
and space debris.

00:13:34.044 --> 00:13:35.212
And here at UT Austin,

00:13:35.245 --> 00:13:37.881
we have one of the world's
leading experts in this area,

00:13:37.914 --> 00:13:39.349
that's Moriba Jah.

00:13:39.382 --> 00:13:43.987
Moriba is building digital twins
for space domain awareness.

00:13:44.554 --> 00:13:47.891
If we think about the environment
and geosciences, again,

00:13:47.924 --> 00:13:50.627
digital twins could play such a role here.

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:54.731
This picture you see,
Omar Ghattas's, high-resolution,

00:13:54.764 --> 00:13:57.634
physics-based model
of the Antarctic ice sheet,

00:13:57.667 --> 00:14:02.038
which is put together with observational
data of all different kinds

00:14:02.038 --> 00:14:03.840
to understand what might be going on,

00:14:03.874 --> 00:14:07.677
to help guide decisions
about where to drill ice cores,

00:14:07.711 --> 00:14:09.312
where to take observations,

00:14:09.346 --> 00:14:13.316
and ultimately to inform the 
decision-making around our future climate.

00:14:14.251 --> 00:14:16.620
We see also here the work of Clint Dawson

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.256
in building a digital twin
of a coastal area,

00:14:19.256 --> 00:14:22.792
here, the Gulf Coast,

00:14:22.826 --> 00:14:27.564
again combining powerful physics models
with all the different kinds of data

00:14:27.564 --> 00:14:30.500
and here, focused on making
storm surge modeling

00:14:30.533 --> 00:14:32.802
for hurricanes even more accurate,

00:14:32.802 --> 00:14:35.705
again, in support
of critical decision-making.

00:14:36.506 --> 00:14:37.841
And then in medicine,

00:14:37.841 --> 00:14:39.910
I think it's pretty clear
that digital twins

00:14:39.943 --> 00:14:41.344
have such a role to play

00:14:41.378 --> 00:14:44.314
in realizing the promise
of personalized medicine.

00:14:44.948 --> 00:14:47.117
Here we see some of the work
of Michael Sacks

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.653
from our Oden Institute Willerson Center,

00:14:49.653 --> 00:14:52.188
in moving towards patient-specific,

00:14:52.222 --> 00:14:54.291
personalized heart care,

00:14:54.291 --> 00:14:56.927
and the work of Tom Yankeelov
and David Hellmuth,

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:59.329
also in the Oden Institute,

00:14:59.329 --> 00:15:03.867
also working with Dell Medical School
and part of biomedical engineering,

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:06.937
in building digital twins
for cancer patients.

00:15:07.537 --> 00:15:10.473
So I hope that helps to, as I said,

00:15:10.507 --> 00:15:13.576
provoke your imaginations to think
about what might be possible.

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:17.213
I personally could not be more excited

00:15:17.247 --> 00:15:21.551
about a future world where digital
twins are enabling safer,

00:15:21.584 --> 00:15:24.020
more efficient engineering systems.

00:15:24.054 --> 00:15:27.557
They're enabling a better understanding
of the natural world around us

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:30.160
and they're enabling
better medical outcomes

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:31.962
for all of us as an individual.

00:15:31.962 --> 00:15:33.163
Thank you.

00:15:33.196 --> 00:15:35.966
(Applause)

